// endpoint_select_in_editor.proto — SelectInEditor: a HOST-RAISED switch. WEB
// section. The rail's row click calls this rather than SelectWorkspace, so a
// switch made in a page is the EDITOR'S OWN SWITCH, exactly as fast as a key.
//
// WHY THE EDITOR MAKES IT. Emacs moves its frame the moment it requests a
// switch (a tab chord, a picker, a banner click), and the roster's `current`
// only confirms it. A page that called SelectWorkspace itself left the frame to
// follow the roster push the selection provokes, which waits on the whole
// registry being re-read: a sidebar click took some 350ms where the same switch
// by key took 10 (2026-10-23). Relayed, the click reaches Emacs as the host
// stream's `select_requested`, and Emacs requests the switch through the same
// path every key takes.
//
// A WORKSPACE THE EDITOR DOES NOT HOLD IS SELECTED HERE. Emacs holds a host
// stream for every workspace it has a tab for, and a row can be clicked
// without one (a closed row, an open one whose tab has not opened yet). The
// event is handed to exactly the host streams standing at that moment, and
// when there are none the daemon selects the workspace itself, as
// SelectWorkspace would, so no click goes nowhere.

